<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Cornwall</h1><p class="st-meta">710 CE – 936 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last surviving British kingdom of the south-west peninsula, which held out against Wessex until the 9th and 10th centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> emerged as a separate British kingdom as the larger kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dumnonia">Dumnonia</a> shrank under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">West Saxon</a> pressure. By the early 8th century <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Wessex</a> had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, and the British realm beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tamar">Tamar</a> became known as Kernow or Cornwall. Its kings are poorly recorded; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geraint-of-dumnonia">Geraint</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex">Ine of Wessex</a> around 710.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> kept its Brittonic language, saints' cults and links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Brittany</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">Tin</a> mining and sea trade continued from Roman times. A defeat of a combined Cornish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hingston-down">Hingston Down</a> in 838 shows its kings still resisting the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annales-cambriae">Annales Cambriae</a> record the drowning of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dungarth">Dungarth</a> in 875.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> was brought under English control by the 9th century, and Aethelstan fixed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tamar">Tamar</a> as the boundary around 936. It became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England</a>, while the Cornish language survived into the 18th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-cornwall.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Cornwall" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Chris Downer · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lostwithiel_-_the_Stannary_Palace_-_geograph.org.uk_-_571361.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>710 CE – 936 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Geraint, Dungarth</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cornish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kernow, Cerniw</dd></dl></aside></div>
